\section{Why long, and why not a list of \code{m} frames}{


\link{hvti_imputation}'s own record is a matrix rather than a count because the
record is meant to reduce, never to require reconstruction. The same
reasoning picks the storage shape here. A long frame -- one \code{.imp} column
naming the draw, one \code{.id} column naming the original row, then every
column of the input -- reduces to a single completed dataset with one
\code{imputation == 1} filter, and is already the shape a correct downstream
analysis expects: fit a model to each draw, then pool the fits. A list of
\code{m} frames does not reduce to anything without picking an element out of
the list first. The long form is also the shape \code{mice::complete(imp, "long")} already returns, and the shape SAS's \verb{PROC MI OUT=} produces --
unforced parity with both.
}

\section{Why the matrix and the two row-level columns are not lists of \code{m}}{


Which cells were originally missing does not change across the \code{m}
draws -- only what they were filled \emph{with} does -- so \code{\link[=imputed_matrix]{imputed_matrix()}}
is identical for every draw and is stored once. \code{\link[=complete_case_pass]{complete_case_pass()}} is
read from the input before any imputation runs, so it is the same for
every draw by construction. \code{\link[=analysis_pass]{analysis_pass()}} is less obvious but still
invariant: \code{mice} guarantees every column that entered the imputation is
complete in every draw, so completeness over \code{cc_vars} evaluates
identically regardless of which draw's values are substituted in. Storing
three lists of \code{m} identical vectors would work, but it would misstate the
record -- it would let a caller ask a question ("does row 12's
analysis-pass status differ by draw?") that cannot have a "yes" for any
